 key squares, this could lead to a strong attack.\n\nReason: Knight to h2 delivers check to the exposed White king on h3. This is the most forcing move available - it gains tempo by forcing White's response, and the knight will be well-placed after the king moves. With my bishop already controlling dark squares near White's king and the knight adding pressure, I can build a strong attack against the exposed king. Checking moves that force the opponent's hand are generally strong when the enemy king is exposed.\n\nAnswer: N: g4-h2"}
